{"name":"Rapeseed Oil","hex":"#a69425","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rapeseed oil","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick brownish-yellow oil expressed from rapeseed, used chiefly for cooking, lubricating and in the manufacture of soap and india rubber."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rapeseed_oil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rapeseed oil","blocks":[{"text":"Rapeseed oil is one of the oldest known vegetable oils. There are both edible and industrial forms produced from rapeseed, the seed of several cultivars of the plant family Brassicaceae (mustards). The term \"rapeseed\" applies to oilseeds from the species Brassica napus and Brassica rapa, while the term canola refers to specific rapeseed varieties bred to produce oil for use in human and animal foods. Canola refers to a cultivar developed by Canadian researchers in the mid 1970s. In manufacturing, the edible varieties of canola are required to contain less than 2% erucic acid in Canada, the United States, European Union, and many other countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Canola_Oil_%284107885913%29_Cropped.jpg/330px-Canola_Oil_%284107885913%29_Cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapeseed_oil"},{"title":"Rapeseed","blocks":[{"text":"Rapeseed, also known as rape and oilseed rape and canola, is a yellow-flowered member of the Brassicaceae family."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Brassica_napus_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-169.jpg/330px-Brassica_napus_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-169.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapeseed"},{"title":"Oil","blocks":[{"text":"Oil is a liquid with varying degrees of viscosity depending on temperature. Oil is any nonpolar chemical substance that is composed primarily of hydrocarbons and is hydrophobic and lipophilic. Oils are usually flammable and surface active. Most oils are unsaturated lipids that are liquid at room temperature."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oil"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Waldo_Hills_Drive_-_DPLA_-_65febc8b1ee46c1437870b42a0eb9606.jpg/500px-Waldo_Hills_Drive_-_DPLA_-_65febc8b1ee46c1437870b42a0eb9606.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Waldo_Hills_Drive_-_DPLA_-_65febc8b1ee46c1437870b42a0eb9606.jpg/1280px-Waldo_Hills_Drive_-_DPLA_-_65febc8b1ee46c1437870b42a0eb9606.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Waldo_Hills_Drive_-_DPLA_-_65febc8b1ee46c1437870b42a0eb9606.jpg/500px-Waldo_Hills_Drive_-_DPLA_-_65febc8b1ee46c1437870b42a0eb9606.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Canola field","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWaldo_Hills_Drive_-_DPLA_-_65febc8b1ee46c1437870b42a0eb9606.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/CanolaBlooms.JPG/120px-CanolaBlooms.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/CanolaBlooms.JPG/250px-CanolaBlooms.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/CanolaBlooms.JPG/120px-CanolaBlooms.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanolaBlooms.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Canola_Flower.jpg/120px-Canola_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons